 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "Define efficiency",
   "text": "Efficiency is the fraction of the heat taken in that comes out as useful work: \\(\\eta=W/Q_1\\). Over a cycle \\(\\Delta U=0\\), so \\(W=Q_1-Q_2\\), which gives \\(\\eta=1-Q_2/Q_1\\).",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6000
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Use Q₂/Q₁ = T₂/T₁",
   "text": "The last derivation gave \\(Q_2/Q_1=T_2/T_1\\). Substitute: \\(\\eta=1-\\dfrac{T_2}{T_1}\\), with both temperatures in kelvin. Only the two reservoir temperatures matter.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6000
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Read the rectangle",
   "text": "The big box is \\(Q_1\\). The blue bottom part (height \\(T_2\\)) is the heat \\(Q_2\\) thrown away; the orange top part (height \\(T_1-T_2\\)) is the work. The efficiency is the **fraction of the box that is orange**: \\(\\dfrac{T_1-T_2}{T_1}\\). Here \\(\\eta={{eta*100|1}}\\%\\).",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6500
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "Efficiency curve",
   "text": "Keep \\(T_2={{T2|0}}\\) K and raise \\(T_1\\): \\(\\eta\\) climbs, but more and more slowly, and **never reaches 100 %**. Drag \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\) and watch the dot.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6500
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "Lower T₂ or raise T₁?",
   "text": "Try the same 50 K both ways. Raising \\(T_1\\) by 50 K gives \\(\\eta={{e50*100|1}}\\%\\); lowering \\(T_2\\) by 50 K gives \\(\\eta={{e50b*100|1}}\\%\\). Lowering \\(T_2\\) wins, because \\(T_2/T_1\\) changes by \\(\\Delta/T_1\\) when \\(T_2\\) falls by \\(\\Delta\\), but only by \\(\\dfrac{T_2}{T_1}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\Delta}{T_1+\\Delta}\\) (smaller, as \\(T_2<T_1\\)) when \\(T_1\\) rises.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 8000
  },
  {
   "id": "s5",
   "title": "Why never 100 %?",
   "text": "\\(\\eta=1\\) needs \\(T_2=0\\) K or \\(T_1\\to\\infty\\); neither can ever be reached. So even a perfect, frictionless engine must throw some heat \\(Q_2\\) away.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6000
  }
 ],
